00:00:56.989 --> 00:01:01.530
Center, which is looking precisely at areas
of scientific reproducibility, has an endowed

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:07.789
chair in the Center for Disease Prevention
at Stanford University, and is someone who

00:01:07.789 --> 00:01:11.389
at Stanford is ubiquitous and someone whom
everyone respects.

00:01:11.389 --> 00:01:13.719
And I'm really looking forward to hearing
what he has to say this morning.

00:01:13.719 --> 00:01:18.890
John, it's up to you
Thank you, Mark, It's a great pleasure to

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:27.450
participate in this series and to try to discuss
a pretty convoluted topic of reproducibility.

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:30.450
There's a lot of confusing terminology to
start with.

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:34.909
People are talking about reproducibility,
but they're also talking about replication.

00:01:34.909 --> 00:01:41.090
And then, you can hear exact replication,
and conceptual replication, reanalysis, repeatability,

00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:42.920
corroboration, triangulation.

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:47.310
All these terms have been out there for a
long time, but they're currently being revisited

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:52.770
as we are facing new challenges and new dilemmas
with new types of studies, new types of data,

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:56.359
and new challenges on what reproducibility
means.

00:01:56.359 --> 00:02:05.020
This is showing how often the term reproducibility
is used across all domains of science.

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:11.200
There's an exponential growth over time in
the popularity of this terminology.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:17.440
Basically, we can think of three types of
reproducibility-- reproducibility of methods,

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:21.860
reproducibility of results, and reproducibility
of inferences.

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:25.920
Reproducibility of methods is the ability
to understand or repeat as exactly as possible

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:28.150
the experimental and computational procedures.

00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:35.020
So if you have some software and some script,
if you use the same software and the same

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:38.920
script, you should be able to get the same
conclusion if you also have the same data.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:45.160
So it's an issue of performing a reanalysis
or kind of repeating and getting the same

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:48.760
result out of the same package.

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:50.680
Sometimes it may not be exactly the same result.

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:57.120
So for example, if you have a permutation
procedure and you run it two different times,

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:01.320
you may not get exactly the same result; but
if the result is substantially different,

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:05.480
then you should start to worry about something
not being exactly what you thought it should

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:07.150
be.

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:12.240
Reproducibility of results is something that
usually we use the term "replication," which

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:14.010
means that we're performing a new study.

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:19.790
We are collecting new data, new observations,
new participants, new whatever.

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:22.540
And we
want to produce corroborating results in that

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:29.120
new experiment having followed the same observational
or experimental method as we did in the original

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:30.120
study.

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:34.790
So people will talk about exact replication
when they try to do exactly the same thing.

00:03:34.790 --> 00:03:37.550
Obviously, it's never possible to do exactly
the same thing.

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:43.430
There will always be some deviation from what
was done in the first study-- minor or major

00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:44.990
sometimes.

00:03:44.990 --> 00:03:50.590
And therefore, this leads to potential for
debate, and this leads to reproducibility

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:56.530
of inferences, which is the making of knowledge
claims of similar strengths from a study replication,

00:03:56.530 --> 00:04:00.319
or from multiple studies, or from a body of
evidence.

00:04:00.319 --> 00:04:03.459
Different people see the same results, and
they reach different conclusions on what these

00:04:03.459 --> 00:04:10.040
mean because of different backgrounds, different
conflicts of interest, different beliefs,

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:15.460
different priors, or different understanding
on what the results mean.

00:04:15.460 --> 00:04:20.680
There's differences across scientific fields
that effect also what we mean by reproducible

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:22.240
research.

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:27.949
It's not the same in sociology, in physics,
in medicine, in bioinformatics.

00:04:27.949 --> 00:04:34.110
There's a great variance in the degree of
determinism that these fields have.

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:38.540
The signal to measurement error ratio and
can be substantially different.

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:44.800
The complexity of the designs and the measurement
tools can also vary a lot.

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:49.180
The closeness of fit that we are seeking between
the hypothesis and the experimental design

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:51.919
and data may also be different.

00:04:51.919 --> 00:04:57.020
The statistical and analytical methods that
are used to test hypotheses-- some of them

00:04:57.020 --> 00:05:05.740
get entrenched within statistic scientific
heterogeneity of experimental results may

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:11.120
vary-- usually it's higher in social science
disciplines than in biomedical disciplines

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:15.530
and higher in biomedical disciplines compared
to physical sciences.

00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:20.990
Well, if there is even a culture of replication
transparency, and cumulating knowledge, some

00:05:20.990 --> 00:05:28.270
fields are very open, and they're sharing
software, code, data, protocols, as a [? pinpoint

00:05:28.270 --> 00:05:29.270
?] [? on.

00:05:29.270 --> 00:05:32.949
?] Others don't share much or anything.

00:05:32.949 --> 00:05:37.939
Some fields are willing to perform replications
and they feel that unless they do that, it's

00:05:37.939 --> 00:05:40.990
not going to be believable no matter what
they come up with.

00:05:40.990 --> 00:05:44.680
In other fields, the replication [? is ?] a
[? me-too ?] waste of time.

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:50.710
The statistical criteria of truth claims can
vary substantially.

00:05:50.710 --> 00:05:57.979
Many fields in biomedicine are stuck with
p-values less than 0.05, being enough to claim

00:05:57.979 --> 00:06:05.150
a discovery or some sort of finding while
in-- [? omits ?] p-values of 10 to the minus

00:06:05.150 --> 00:06:08.900
8 are sought pretty much routinely.

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:12.560
Some fields are completely abandoning p-values.

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:16.110
They're using a false discovery rate or base
factors.

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:22.439
And some fields are in transition using different
modalities that sometimes reach different

00:06:22.439 --> 00:06:25.939
conclusions when it comes to reproducibility
of inferences.

00:06:25.939 --> 00:06:30.539
Finally, there's differences to the purposes
to which findings will be put in the consequences

00:06:30.539 --> 00:06:31.589
of false conclusions.

00:06:31.589 --> 00:06:43.639
So some scientific findings may be interested
in some very fancy mechanistic view of life

00:06:43.639 --> 00:06:48.150
or some phenomena,
and this may have very little repercussions

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:50.689
for life and death.

00:06:50.689 --> 00:06:58.339
But other studies may make inputs into accommodations
that affect the treatment of millions of people

00:06:58.339 --> 00:07:01.659
and obviously, their outcomes-- including
life and death.

00:07:01.659 --> 00:07:07.490
This is a map of 20 million papers published
in the last 15 years.

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:15.999
It also includes two million [? patterns,
?] and it's an effort to see how many solar

00:07:15.999 --> 00:07:19.500
systems exist in this galaxy of science.

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:27.199
There's about 200,000 solar systems of different
scientific disciplines and the different colors

00:07:27.199 --> 00:07:29.449
highlight the 13 big clusters.

00:07:29.449 --> 00:07:33.009
So life sciences is one such and physics is
another.

00:07:33.009 --> 00:07:38.789
But if you look closely, there is 200,000
disciplines, and each one of them may have

00:07:38.789 --> 00:07:45.490
its own rules of engagement and its own understanding
about what reproducibility means and how exactly

00:07:45.490 --> 00:07:46.879
it should be implemented.

00:07:46.879 --> 00:07:52.819
Now when you try to cross the boundaries,
sometimes you see that fields that are seemingly

00:07:52.819 --> 00:07:56.870
doing almost the same thing, they have very
different standards about their reproducibility

00:07:56.870 --> 00:07:58.449
practices.

00:07:58.449 --> 00:08:04.499
The common denominator across almost all fields
is that everybody is trying to come up with

00:08:04.499 --> 00:08:05.580
a significant result.

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:13.580
So this is an evaluation of the entire PubMed
worth of 15 million papers since 1990 that

00:08:13.580 --> 00:08:17.639
we performed and published last year in JAMA.

00:08:17.639 --> 00:08:23.610
And we found that 96% of papers that used
p-values in their abstracts-- they claimed

00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:26.009
significant results-- statistically significant
results.

00:08:26.009 --> 00:08:33.740
Looking at about a million full-text papers
in PMC, again, 96% of them claimed significant

00:08:33.740 --> 00:08:35.930
results and the vast majority of them say
that they're novel.

00:08:35.930 --> 00:08:40.630
So if you take that literally, scientific
discovery has become a boring nuisance.

00:08:40.630 --> 00:08:45.760
There's just so much of it that we don't really
know what to make of it.

00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:53.270
So reproducibility is probably our best chance
at the moment to filter out how many of these

00:08:53.270 --> 00:08:59.940
millions of claims are real, true, and potentially
translatable to something that could help

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:02.220
people eventually.

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:07.560
Now in the current environment, this may not
be easy because the typical recipe of research

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:14.190
practices in most biomedical fields is that
you have the prototype of the principal investigator,

00:09:14.190 --> 00:09:16.150
who is solo and siloed.

00:09:16.150 --> 00:09:22.110
Unavoidably, this means that he or she will
be using mostly studies of small sample sizes

00:09:22.110 --> 00:09:27.690
and would be cherry picking one or the best
hypothesis to try to get two significant results.

00:09:27.690 --> 00:09:30.580
Because this is the way to then seek funding.

00:09:30.580 --> 00:09:33.540
There's a lot of post-hoc analysis being done.

00:09:33.540 --> 00:09:38.440
P-value of 0.05 or slightly less is enough
as I said before.

00:09:38.440 --> 00:09:41.510
There's no registration of the ideas or the
protocols.

00:09:41.510 --> 00:09:49.940
Very little data sharing and no replication,
because replication only has a chance of refuting

00:09:49.940 --> 00:09:53.520
or not replicating something that is already
statistically significant.

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:58.370
So people think, why should I waste my time
to kill all the work that I did and then not

00:09:58.370 --> 00:10:01.350
be able to publish it in a high-profile journal?

00:10:01.350 --> 00:10:09.300
So this is a setting of incentives that don't
really align with reproducible science.

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:14.820
And this is what you get when you have a field
performing in that fashion.

00:10:14.820 --> 00:10:21.740
This is looking at about 1800 datasets of
the fMRI studies, and I have plotted for you

00:10:21.740 --> 00:10:25.120
the number of foci that have
been discovered as a function of the sample

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:26.120
size.

00:10:26.120 --> 00:10:31.320
The only way to discover a lot of foci for
different phenotypes, or features, or tasks

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:33.760
is to perform very small studies.

00:10:33.760 --> 00:10:37.199
When we perform larger studies, these foci
go away.

00:10:37.199 --> 00:10:38.470
The facts become much smaller.

00:10:38.470 --> 00:10:45.140
Most of those disappear, and it's very likely
that most of these claims' discoveries in

00:10:45.140 --> 00:10:48.160
very small studies don't really mean much.

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:51.960
This is not just happening in fMRI research.

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:56.780
A couple of years ago, we looked across very
different fields across neuroscience ranging

00:10:56.780 --> 00:11:02.060
from early basic research, preclinical research,
clinical research, clinical trials, and the

00:11:02.060 --> 00:11:03.850
common denominator was power failure.

00:11:03.850 --> 00:11:10.060
We had a paradigm of having a very small sample
size studies that you expect to have major

00:11:10.060 --> 00:11:11.530
problems with their reproducibility.

00:11:11.530 --> 00:11:18.960
This is another exercise looking at neuroscience,
psychology, medical psychology, and psychiatry

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:25.260
literature-where we did some in-depth text
mining of these studies.

00:11:25.260 --> 00:11:30.700
And the common denominator-- even though there
is quite some variability across different

00:11:30.700 --> 00:11:37.730
subfields and journals-- is that they have
very small sample sizes, which are likely

00:11:37.730 --> 00:11:42.810
to lead to inflated effect sizes and very
often false positive effect sizes, which you

00:11:42.810 --> 00:11:49.430
need some replication effort to try to see
whether they stand the test of replication.

00:11:49.430 --> 00:11:50.950
This is a completely different field.

00:11:50.950 --> 00:11:51.970
This is economics.

00:11:51.970 --> 00:11:56.810
Along with Tom Stanley and [? Chris ?] [INAUDIBLE]
we've looked at every single meta-analysis

00:11:56.810 --> 00:11:58.550
that we could identify in economics.

00:11:58.550 --> 00:12:02.750
This is mostly observational economics as
opposed to experimental economics.

00:12:02.750 --> 00:12:08.550
We found 130 topics, more than 10,000 studies,
and more than 70,000 effect estimates.

00:12:08.550 --> 00:12:14.930
The common denominator is, again, very small,
very limited power for the majority of [? p

00:12:14.930 --> 00:12:16.410
?] studies and these topics.

00:12:16.410 --> 00:12:21.920
So you expect to see a similar performance
of observational economics and observational

00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:25.840
neuroscience, even though these two disciplines
are so far apart.

00:12:25.840 --> 00:12:32.190
There are some fields where we do have the
benefit of having performed replications in

00:12:32.190 --> 00:12:33.880
very large scale.

00:12:33.880 --> 00:12:36.090
And one such field is genetic epidemiology.

00:12:36.090 --> 00:12:42.340
Because in genetic epidemiology, the cost
of performing replications has been very low.

00:12:42.340 --> 00:12:46.640
It's very easy and very cheap to genotype
very large sample sizes.

00:12:46.640 --> 00:12:51.660
And this is in contrast to what is happening
in many other fields where running the experiments

00:12:51.660 --> 00:12:53.600
continues to be very expensive.

00:12:53.600 --> 00:12:59.450
So in genetics, we could go back when we moved
into the genome wide association era and test

00:12:59.450 --> 00:13:03.940
what we have discovered in the candidate gene
era when we were performing research with

00:13:03.940 --> 00:13:09.440
small studies one gene at a time, one hypothesis
at a time, or a few hypotheses at a time.

00:13:09.440 --> 00:13:13.000
Their replication rate was 1.2%.

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:18.320
So of all these several tens of thousands
of papers that have been published in the

00:13:18.320 --> 00:13:24.410
candidate gene era about associations of single
genes tested against single phenotypes, only

00:13:24.410 --> 00:13:29.880
1.2% were validated when they were tested
with very large sample sizes with very accurate

00:13:29.880 --> 00:13:35.180
and large-scale genome-wide association studies.

00:13:35.180 --> 00:13:41.730
Preclinical research has also seen a flurry
of results that are not looking very nice

00:13:41.730 --> 00:13:43.100
in terms of their reproducibility.

00:13:43.100 --> 00:13:45.750
Preclinical research has also seen a flurry
of results that are not looking very nice

00:13:45.750 --> 00:13:46.750
in terms of their reproducibility.

00:13:46.750 --> 00:13:51.630
A series of investigations, mostly run by
the industry, have shown reproducibility rates

00:13:51.630 --> 00:13:54.440
ranging from 0% to 25%.

00:13:54.440 --> 00:13:59.980
The reason that the industry has led these
efforts is that they do want to use preclinical

00:13:59.980 --> 00:14:03.240
research to try to develop new drug targets.

00:14:03.240 --> 00:14:09.400
So they use these leads from papers that have
been published by the best investigators in

00:14:09.400 --> 00:14:14.290
academia, in the best centers, the best research
institutes, and they want to make sense of

00:14:14.290 --> 00:14:19.420
them and translate them into new drugs or
new diagnostics, and it doesn't work.

00:14:19.420 --> 00:14:27.360
So when they did that in large-scale research,
25% of these high-profile papers could be

00:14:27.360 --> 00:14:28.370
reproduced.

00:14:28.370 --> 00:14:33.950
So Glenn Begley wrote in Nature in 2012 that
the failure to win the war on cancer has been

00:14:33.950 --> 00:14:37.170
blamed on many factors; but recently, a new
culprit has emerged.

00:14:37.170 --> 00:14:40.240
Too many basic scientific discoveries are
wrong.

00:14:40.240 --> 00:14:45.460
And that was his conclusion when his team
was able to reproduce only six of 53 landmark

00:14:45.460 --> 00:14:50.500
studies for Amgen oncology drug [? target
?] projects.

00:14:50.500 --> 00:14:54.030
Now what does it mean to replicate or to reproduce?

00:14:54.030 --> 00:15:03.130
This can be contested, obviously, and we don't
really know whether we have the right answer

00:15:03.130 --> 00:15:08.740
when we have an original study, or when we
have a replication reproduction effort that

00:15:08.740 --> 00:15:12.320
disagrees in one way or another versus the
original study.

00:15:12.320 --> 00:15:16.610
It's possible that the original study may
be correct, and the reproducibility effort

00:15:16.610 --> 00:15:17.610
may be wrong.

00:15:17.610 --> 00:15:22.350
It could be that both are correct, but they
were not really exact replicas of each other.

00:15:22.350 --> 00:15:28.340
Or it could be that both of them are actually
wrong and there is some common biases that

00:15:28.340 --> 00:15:29.340
they had.

00:15:29.340 --> 00:15:32.640
So regardless of whether they agree or they
disagree, they could still be wrong.

00:15:32.640 --> 00:15:39.160
But at a minimum, when we have large numbers
of studies, we can start talking about patterns

00:15:39.160 --> 00:15:45.520
of reproducibility across different fields,
even though single cases will be heavily debated.

00:15:45.520 --> 00:15:48.220
And this is what they call the reproducibility
wars.

00:15:48.220 --> 00:15:56.310
So pretty recently, the first results of the
reproducibility project Cancer Biology were

00:15:56.310 --> 00:15:57.400
released.

00:15:57.400 --> 00:16:02.400
And every single one of these reproducibility
efforts was heavily contested.

00:16:02.400 --> 00:16:07.660
So here's one example where the results look
very nice.

00:16:07.660 --> 00:16:12.260
You have an effect in the original study that
is significant; you have an effect in the

00:16:12.260 --> 00:16:13.260
replication effort.

00:16:13.260 --> 00:16:16.120
The meta-analysis also shows an effect.

00:16:16.120 --> 00:16:21.030
The effect size is slightly less in the reproducibility,
but that's OK.

00:16:21.030 --> 00:16:27.350
But depending on whether you are just, for
example, for multiplicity of analysis, then

00:16:27.350 --> 00:16:31.070
the result in the reproducibility may no longer
be statistically significant.

00:16:31.070 --> 00:16:37.200
So one might say that we have powered the
reproducibility to be significant, and now

00:16:37.200 --> 00:16:38.200
it is not.

00:16:38.200 --> 00:16:41.910
But if you don't correct [? for ?] multiplicity
comparison, which is also fair, because if

00:16:41.910 --> 00:16:44.750
this is a reproducibility effort, then it
is significant.

00:16:44.750 --> 00:16:48.540
This led to a huge debate, even though I don't
really see much debate.

00:16:48.540 --> 00:16:51.690
These results seem to be pretty consistent
with each other.

00:16:51.690 --> 00:16:55.140
Here's another example where things are clearly
very different.

00:16:55.140 --> 00:17:01.040
I mean, you see that if you take panel A,
the effect size, but also the confidence intervals

00:17:01.040 --> 00:17:03.770
are very different in the original study.

00:17:03.770 --> 00:17:11.330
In the repeatability estimate, you have a
glass delta of 7.41 versus a minus 0.32 and

00:17:11.330 --> 00:17:12.920
with pretty tight confidence intervals.

00:17:12.920 --> 00:17:18.800
But again, this can lead to a lot of debate,
and there's lots of things at stake here.

00:17:18.800 --> 00:17:24.900
It's not just the veracity of the results,
but it's also whatever other investment has

00:17:24.900 --> 00:17:31.310
been made into trying to develop the original
study into some translational application.

00:17:31.310 --> 00:17:35.630
For example, whether new studies have been
launched, whether clinical trials are being

00:17:35.630 --> 00:17:40.700
planned based on these results, whether there
is investment, whether entrepreneurs or venture

00:17:40.700 --> 00:17:47.000
capitalists are thinking about giving money
to invest in that original observation that

00:17:47.000 --> 00:17:50.410
now seems to be so squarely refuted.

00:17:50.410 --> 00:17:54.030
These concerns have been raised increasingly
in a number of fields.

00:17:54.030 --> 00:18:00.350
Here's a partial list showing reproducibility
concerns in preclinical studies, on microarray

00:18:00.350 --> 00:18:06.690
data, on stroke animal studies [? general
?] biology, oncology, genome sequencing, mouse

00:18:06.690 --> 00:18:10.730
in-vivo studies, genealogical studies, mouse
models, genomic [? cell line ?] analysis,

00:18:10.730 --> 00:18:16.860
microarray data, commercial antibodies, and
ELISA, and the way that journals handle information

00:18:16.860 --> 00:18:17.930
also.

00:18:17.930 --> 00:18:23.980
And they pertain to disciplines as different
as science from ecology to genomic bioinformatics,

00:18:23.980 --> 00:18:29.430
stem cell biology, oncology, in vitro testing,
chemistry-led discovery, computational biology,

00:18:29.430 --> 00:18:35.130
pathology biomarkers, organizational psychology,
and observational research of all sorts.

00:18:35.130 --> 00:18:42.260
So my field, if I had to pick one out of many
where I have failed, is probably epidemiology.

00:18:42.260 --> 00:18:51.370
And you can see here that zero out of 52 hypotheses
that were claimed in major epidemiological

00:18:51.370 --> 00:18:54.260
studies were subsequently confirmed in randomized
trials.

00:18:54.260 --> 00:18:59.330
This is a nice paper by Stan Young and Carr
a few years ago.

00:18:59.330 --> 00:19:02.980
This is one of the most famous recent reproducibility
attempts.

00:19:02.980 --> 00:19:11.110
It's the open science collaboration effort
where 270 investigators and their teams try

00:19:11.110 --> 00:19:17.050
to reproduce 100 experiments published in
the top psychology journals.

00:19:17.050 --> 00:19:20.010
And the results clearly don't look very nice.

00:19:20.010 --> 00:19:24.730
You can see the comparison of p-values and
the effect sizes in the original studies and

00:19:24.730 --> 00:19:25.730
their replications.

00:19:25.730 --> 00:19:31.080
About a third of the influential papers could
be reproduced.

00:19:31.080 --> 00:19:36.640
Depending on how exactly you define reproducibility,
you may have a little bit of a different answer.

00:19:36.640 --> 00:19:39.770
But clearly, they seem to be different.

00:19:39.770 --> 00:19:44.720
And even when they could be reproduced, the
effect sizes typically were much smaller in

00:19:44.720 --> 00:19:47.740
the replications compared to the original
studies.

00:19:47.740 --> 00:19:51.620
This has led to a reproducibility war.

00:19:51.620 --> 00:19:57.480
So there were technical comments and replies
in science a few months after the publication

00:19:57.480 --> 00:19:59.730
of that very influential study.

00:19:59.730 --> 00:20:03.190
And some top psychologists felt that there's
nothing wrong.

00:20:03.190 --> 00:20:11.670
This is the way that it should be, and actually,
the reproducibility effort is wrong, is not

00:20:11.670 --> 00:20:12.900
what it should be.

00:20:12.900 --> 00:20:14.050
And there's many reasons.

00:20:14.050 --> 00:20:22.050
And there were examples being pursued as really
not being the best efforts to replicate the

00:20:22.050 --> 00:20:23.260
original finding.

00:20:23.260 --> 00:20:28.950
However, one would wonder if you'd have a
situation where you have [? 217 ?] investigators

00:20:28.950 --> 00:20:35.250
and their teams from the best institutions
joining forces performing something with the

00:20:35.250 --> 00:20:40.860
best possible methods with perfect transparency,
with everything being reviewed at all stages

00:20:40.860 --> 00:20:49.330
by the best peers, if this is found or claimed
to be so wrong and so suboptimal, what would

00:20:49.330 --> 00:20:54.750
one think about other studies that really
don't have the benefit of that cumulative

00:20:54.750 --> 00:21:00.290
wisdom
and expertise being put in them?

00:21:00.290 --> 00:21:01.380
Here's another example.

00:21:01.380 --> 00:21:05.110
This is experimental economics.

00:21:05.110 --> 00:21:10.990
They have also tried to do their reproducibility
checks, and their reproducibility results

00:21:10.990 --> 00:21:11.990
seem a bit better.

00:21:11.990 --> 00:21:14.730
About a third of the studies could not be
produced.

00:21:14.730 --> 00:21:16.040
It's a small sample.

00:21:16.040 --> 00:21:21.360
It's only 18 papers that have been published
in major economics journals, but it seems

00:21:21.360 --> 00:21:29.330
a bit better compared to what has been documented
in preclinical research, in biomedicine, and

00:21:29.330 --> 00:21:30.330
in psychology.

00:21:30.330 --> 00:21:35.470
Now, the difference could be just due to chance,
but it could also be due to the fact that

00:21:35.470 --> 00:21:41.270
experimental economics for a number of years
now has followed a number of policies that

00:21:41.270 --> 00:21:47.870
we think-- both based on theory and on some
empirical data-- should lead to better reproducibility.

00:21:47.870 --> 00:21:51.920
So they do have protocols that are communicated.

00:21:51.920 --> 00:21:57.480
They do share all the software and the code,
and all the raw data with other scientists.

00:21:57.480 --> 00:21:59.929
And this is also a prerequisite for publication.

00:21:59.929 --> 00:22:05.780
So you have a culture where there's openness
and transparency, and a lot of thought goes

00:22:05.780 --> 00:22:07.150
into developing protocols.

00:22:07.150 --> 00:22:12.610
Plus, you have a culture where you have experimental
results where you expect to see better reproducibility

00:22:12.610 --> 00:22:17.210
compared to just observational datasets--
especially observational datasets that sometimes

00:22:17.210 --> 00:22:23.610
are not even conceived with a particular purpose
of looking at one specific question that then

00:22:23.610 --> 00:22:25.110
gets published.

00:22:25.110 --> 00:22:30.770
Here's another side of the reproducibility
debate.

00:22:30.770 --> 00:22:36.351
Sometimes effect sizes may not completely
nullifiy upon replication efforts, but they

00:22:36.351 --> 00:22:37.650
may become much smaller.

00:22:37.650 --> 00:22:42.220
And this is one classic example where we see
this.

00:22:42.220 --> 00:22:47.570
In biomarkers studies, if you plot the relative
risk in the highly sited study that typically

00:22:47.570 --> 00:22:51.750
has been one of the first ones or the first
one about a biomarker and the relative risk

00:22:51.750 --> 00:22:57.010
in the largest study, all the action is below
the diagonal with just a couple of exceptions.

00:22:57.010 --> 00:23:01.380
Which means that the largest study finds much
smaller effect sizes.

00:23:01.380 --> 00:23:06.290
They're not null, so they're not a relative
risk of one, but they're much smaller compared

00:23:06.290 --> 00:23:08.340
to what the original study had found.

00:23:08.340 --> 00:23:15.470
So that biomarker still has some ability to
discriminate or predict, but not as much--

00:23:15.470 --> 00:23:21.660
far less compared to what the original highly
sited study would have suggested.

00:23:21.660 --> 00:23:28.080
Another dimension, as I mentioned before,
is reanalysis or repeatability.

00:23:28.080 --> 00:23:33.850
In reanalysis, we're not performing a new
study, we're just going back and re-examining

00:23:33.850 --> 00:23:40.690
the data from a study that has been done,
published, and now everybody can scrutinize

00:23:40.690 --> 00:23:42.780
because the raw data are available.

00:23:42.780 --> 00:23:48.440
So there are some experience of repeatability
checks, and one of them is an effort that

00:23:48.440 --> 00:23:51.590
we did jointly with Nature Genetics a number
of years ago.

00:23:51.590 --> 00:23:56.840
Nature Genetics was one of the first channels
that made the availability of software protocols

00:23:56.840 --> 00:24:02.510
and raw data a prerequisite for publication
for microarray studies.

00:24:02.510 --> 00:24:08.140
And when we did that exercise looking at 18
papers that had been published in Nature Genetics,

00:24:08.140 --> 00:24:11.910
we could not reproduce more than two of the
18 papers.

00:24:11.910 --> 00:24:16.660
Some could be reproduced with discrepancies,
or with discrepancies from process data or

00:24:16.660 --> 00:24:19.890
partially, but most could not be reproduced
at all.

00:24:19.890 --> 00:24:24.360
And the reason for the nonreproduction was
that the data were not available.

00:24:24.360 --> 00:24:27.600
The software was
homemade and had disappeared.

00:24:27.600 --> 00:24:28.670
The methods were unclear.

00:24:28.670 --> 00:24:33.900
So we had several teams of microarray analysts
around the world who couldn't understand exactly

00:24:33.900 --> 00:24:36.330
what was being done in that paper.

00:24:36.330 --> 00:24:41.910
Or the result was different even though everything
was available and the methods were very clear,

00:24:41.910 --> 00:24:46.600
and the data were there, and the software
could be made to work.

00:24:46.600 --> 00:24:50.160
The result was not what had been published
in the original paper.

00:24:50.160 --> 00:24:56.040
So this is probably disquieting because we're
talking about the best field in genetics and

00:24:56.040 --> 00:25:04.340
one of the best in science at large, and the
most transparent field at that time with avant-garde

00:25:04.340 --> 00:25:08.990
ideas about sharing of information, and software,
and methods.

00:25:08.990 --> 00:25:13.390
Here's another field where there has been
some expands on reanalysis.

00:25:13.390 --> 00:25:17.970
Randomized clinical trial data have mostly
not been available to other investigators,

00:25:17.970 --> 00:25:19.750
but this is changing.

00:25:19.750 --> 00:25:25.270
In the past, it was very difficult to get
these data, but currently, there's about 20,000

00:25:25.270 --> 00:25:29.970
clinical trials worth of data that you can
get hold of one way or another.

00:25:29.970 --> 00:25:36.140
Not necessarily completely free access, but
at least you can apply for getting them and

00:25:36.140 --> 00:25:38.810
hopefully you can get them.

00:25:38.810 --> 00:25:45.360
In the past, there were very, very few such
trials, and we found 37 reanalyses that had

00:25:45.360 --> 00:25:50.850
examined the very same question as the original
trial in the very same data.

00:25:50.850 --> 00:25:57.890
And we found that 35% of the time, the reanalysis
reached an entirely different conclusion compared

00:25:57.890 --> 00:25:58.980
to the original paper.

00:25:58.980 --> 00:26:03.870
So the original paper would have claimed that
the patients should be treated, and the reanalysis

00:26:03.870 --> 00:26:05.890
would say, no, they should not be treated.

00:26:05.890 --> 00:26:11.000
Or the type of patients to be treated would
be different in the original paper, versus

00:26:11.000 --> 00:26:16.370
the reanalysis
One might think that this might be some enemies

00:26:16.370 --> 00:26:19.870
of the original investigators who are trying
to put them to shame.

00:26:19.870 --> 00:26:25.980
But actually, almost always, the original
authors were involved also as co-authors in

00:26:25.980 --> 00:26:31.080
the reanalysis, which means that what we have
documented here is likely to be just the tip

00:26:31.080 --> 00:26:32.300
of the iceberg.

00:26:32.300 --> 00:26:38.140
Probably many, many more reanalyses of randomized
trials were done-- possibly in the hundreds

00:26:38.140 --> 00:26:39.960
or even thousands.

00:26:39.960 --> 00:26:46.220
But a key attractor for disseminating and
publishing the results of a reanalysis has

00:26:46.220 --> 00:26:49.260
been the ability to say that, I found something
new.

00:26:49.260 --> 00:26:53.470
So even though these are the same authors,
having found something new and reaching a

00:26:53.470 --> 00:26:58.380
different conclusion might have been an incentive
to publish, which makes things very confusing.

00:26:58.380 --> 00:27:04.820
But this is the way that the publication system
operates apparently.

00:27:04.820 --> 00:27:11.030
There's also an opposite movement moving in
the opposite direction of reproducibility

00:27:11.030 --> 00:27:15.860
and transparency that would facilitate reproducibility
checks and reanalysis.

00:27:15.860 --> 00:27:22.799
And I call this stealth research, which means
that some organizations and some entities

00:27:22.799 --> 00:27:27.760
feel that nothing should be shared, nothing
should be communicated, information should

00:27:27.760 --> 00:27:29.570
be kept as trade secret.

00:27:29.570 --> 00:27:33.550
There's no need to try to replicate; we will
just do things internally.

00:27:33.550 --> 00:27:37.770
We will make sure that they're fine and then
we will [? conquer ?] the world.

00:27:37.770 --> 00:27:43.060
And this is pretty much the example upon which
Theranos was built.

00:27:43.060 --> 00:27:49.300
So when I wrote that paper in JAMA 2 and
1/2 years, Theranos had published about 100

00:27:49.300 --> 00:27:57.010
patents, but practically nothing in the peer-reviewed
literature about its disruptive innovative

00:27:57.010 --> 00:27:58.010
method.

00:27:58.010 --> 00:28:03.059
So I questioned whether this lack of transparency,
which does not allow anyone to replicate what

00:28:03.059 --> 00:28:06.420
they do and reproduce what they do, is the
way to move forward.

00:28:06.420 --> 00:28:11.870
And at that time, they were really very powerful,
but within a few months after that investigation

00:28:11.870 --> 00:28:18.549
by the Wall Street Journal and scrutiny by
federal regulators and other entities, the

00:28:18.549 --> 00:28:19.730
company imploded.

00:28:19.730 --> 00:28:25.240
So stealth research didn't seem to be such
a good idea after all.

00:28:25.240 --> 00:28:31.740
Some potential solutions to improve reversibility-there's
many such solutions, and I have discussed

00:28:31.740 --> 00:28:32.740
some of them already.

00:28:32.740 --> 00:28:37.260
As I was going through these examples, some
of them we know that they would work very

00:28:37.260 --> 00:28:38.260
well.

00:28:38.260 --> 00:28:43.800
Others are very speculated, and we need empirical
evidence as to their efficacy.

00:28:43.800 --> 00:28:47.540
There are some solutions that may sound nice,
but they could cost a lot.

00:28:47.540 --> 00:28:53.740
They could mean that we need to invest a lot
of effort, and therefore, maybe the trade-offs

00:28:53.740 --> 00:28:56.130
are not such that we would recommend them.

00:28:56.130 --> 00:29:03.980
For example, one solution might be to replicate
everything-- just to keep on replicating every

00:29:03.980 --> 00:29:09.820
single item that you see produced in science
or published in the scientific literature--

00:29:09.820 --> 00:29:11.110
probably not a very good investment.

00:29:11.110 --> 00:29:20.480
That would mean that we would need to either
double the research budget or somehow cut

00:29:20.480 --> 00:29:24.520
our investment in half in terms of what new
ideas we could pursue.

00:29:24.520 --> 00:29:31.059
Or another such idea that I think is highly
suspect is, try to audit everything that is

00:29:31.059 --> 00:29:32.059
being done.

00:29:32.059 --> 00:29:35.669
And this means that I would need to clone
myself, and everybody would need to clone

00:29:35.669 --> 00:29:42.330
themselves so that they have the scientists
and the auditor's scientists following as

00:29:42.330 --> 00:29:47.770
their shadow wherever they go in the lab or
in their computer to try to reanalyze and

00:29:47.770 --> 00:29:49.270
reproduce everything that they do.

00:29:49.270 --> 00:29:51.030
Clearly, we cannot do that.

00:29:51.030 --> 00:29:57.669
However, there are many things that we could
do, and here's a few examples that belong

00:29:57.669 --> 00:30:05.690
to 12 families of reproducibility efforts
that may improve the veracity and the truthfulness

00:30:05.690 --> 00:30:09.580
and trustworthiness of the results that we
get.

00:30:09.580 --> 00:30:14.140
One option is to promote replication culture
at large.

00:30:14.140 --> 00:30:17.330
So embed replication in the discovery phase.

00:30:17.330 --> 00:30:20.160
This is what has happened in genetic epidemiology.

00:30:20.160 --> 00:30:25.299
People don't really separate that much discovery
from replication because they understand that

00:30:25.299 --> 00:30:27.680
replication is part of the discovery.

00:30:27.680 --> 00:30:33.950
So this is one out of zillions of Manhattan
plots that you will see in that literature.

00:30:33.950 --> 00:30:40.100
In the past, we thought that we had discovered
zillions of genes that could affect smoking

00:30:40.100 --> 00:30:41.100
behavior.

00:30:41.100 --> 00:30:44.030
And this is what these Manhattan plots are
about.

00:30:44.030 --> 00:30:49.640
In the candidate gene era, there were 359
genes that had been discovered as being associated

00:30:49.640 --> 00:30:51.250
with smoking behavior.

00:30:51.250 --> 00:30:57.179
When we performed large-scale studies where
we had multiple teams replicating each other's

00:30:57.179 --> 00:31:02.890
signings and joining all their data instead
of just publishing them one piece at a time,

00:31:02.890 --> 00:31:07.350
we could only validate one of these 359.

00:31:07.350 --> 00:31:10.050
And we came up with another dozen or so.

00:31:10.050 --> 00:31:15.211
It's better to have a dozen that we are very
certain that they're true, rather than have

00:31:15.211 --> 00:31:18.970
359 that are completely spurious.

00:31:18.970 --> 00:31:24.580
If we do replication, it needs to be inexpensive
and easy to do.

00:31:24.580 --> 00:31:26.490
And then, who is going to do it?

00:31:26.490 --> 00:31:28.450
Is it going to be the same investigators?

00:31:28.450 --> 00:31:30.930
Is it going to be different investigators
of the same school?

00:31:30.930 --> 00:31:35.110
Is it going to be different investigators
of competing theories and hypotheses?

00:31:35.110 --> 00:31:40.920
Do we need combinations of the above in some
easy-to-do problems?

00:31:40.920 --> 00:31:43.920
Maybe we can open replication to the wide
public.

00:31:43.920 --> 00:31:51.070
So lots of mathematics questions can be split
into tens of thousands of computers.

00:31:51.070 --> 00:31:57.169
And people could run them in their own desktop
or laptop, and then accumulate that information

00:31:57.169 --> 00:31:59.040
at some central facility.

00:31:59.040 --> 00:32:04.000
These are questions that cannot be answered
in a generic term.

00:32:04.000 --> 00:32:07.230
They need to be seen on a case-by-case basis.

00:32:07.230 --> 00:32:12.970
I think that there is some general patterns
that if you have the same investigators trying

00:32:12.970 --> 00:32:16.880
to replicate, you unavoidably have some allegiance
bias.

00:32:16.880 --> 00:32:21.100
The same applies if you have investigators
of the same school.

00:32:21.100 --> 00:32:26.580
So one has to ask, how strong is allegiance
bias likely to be?

00:32:26.580 --> 00:32:32.660
Do we have experimental constraints and safeguards
that would alleviate the concern for such

00:32:32.660 --> 00:32:33.660
allegiance bias?

00:32:33.660 --> 00:32:39.440
And if not, then we probably need to think
of different investigators who try to test

00:32:39.440 --> 00:32:44.480
whether the original experiments were correct
and the inferences were valid.

00:32:44.480 --> 00:32:48.040
Registration is another possibility.

00:32:48.040 --> 00:32:54.000
And this means that if I do have a hypothesis
and a protocol, I would like to register it

00:32:54.000 --> 00:32:57.970
upfront so that people would know that this
is what I do.

00:32:57.970 --> 00:33:02.580
And there would be full transparency about
all the steps that I have followed so that

00:33:02.580 --> 00:33:07.050
people would compare notes with what was the
original intention.

00:33:07.050 --> 00:33:12.000
And they could also try to reproduce what
was done at the first place.

00:33:12.000 --> 00:33:14.440
This is not always possible.

00:33:14.440 --> 00:33:18.390
There's a lot of research I call level-zero
research, blue-sky science.

00:33:18.390 --> 00:33:24.330
There can be no registration there because
really, no one has a preconceived deliverable

00:33:24.330 --> 00:33:25.669
or hypothesis in mind.

00:33:25.669 --> 00:33:30.831
People are just testing like crazy very different
things, and if something pans out, they will

00:33:30.831 --> 00:33:31.940
report it.

00:33:31.940 --> 00:33:34.830
The only thing that I want in that case is
some transparency.

00:33:34.830 --> 00:33:36.950
That this is how this emerged.

00:33:36.950 --> 00:33:43.710
That it was completely unrestricted, exploratory
blue-sky science, and there was no plan, this

00:33:43.710 --> 00:33:44.710
is what I got.

00:33:44.710 --> 00:33:45.710
Now.

00:33:45.710 --> 00:33:47.200
We need some replication efforts.

00:33:47.200 --> 00:33:53.830
But we can't really claim that there was any
specific hypothesis or protocol that was preset.

00:33:53.830 --> 00:33:59.750
Level 1 would be a registration of a dataset,
which means that I can share information on

00:33:59.750 --> 00:34:03.090
how many observational datasets I have available.

00:34:03.090 --> 00:34:09.690
People would know what the spectrum of multiplicity
is in these datasets.

00:34:09.690 --> 00:34:15.409
So when they see a published analysis about
one risk factor with one outcome, they can

00:34:15.409 --> 00:34:21.200
immediately tell that that was from a dataset
that includes five million variables, or a

00:34:21.200 --> 00:34:23.290
dataset that includes five variables.

00:34:23.290 --> 00:34:27.720
That makes a huge difference because if I
have published that analysis with a p-value

00:34:27.720 --> 00:34:33.679
of 0.049 from a database that excludes five
million variables, then people might easily

00:34:33.679 --> 00:34:38.810
question whether that p-value is really valid
And unless I have preregistered that, it's

00:34:38.810 --> 00:34:43.909
very hard to believe that I just closed my
eyes and did not analyze anything else other

00:34:43.909 --> 00:34:46.129
than that association.

00:34:46.129 --> 00:34:50.399
Level 2 would be registration of the protocol;
and that means that there is a protocol.

00:34:50.399 --> 00:34:55.480
Level 3 is registration of the analysis plan;
And that means that there is an analysis plan.

00:34:55.480 --> 00:34:59.369
Very often there is a protocol, but there
is no statistical analysis plan.

00:34:59.369 --> 00:35:03.520
And this is decided at a late stage after
the data have been collected; and sometimes,

00:35:03.520 --> 00:35:10.550
even after some analysis has been done, which
creates some shade of gray into the credibility

00:35:10.550 --> 00:35:11.849
of the research.

00:35:11.849 --> 00:35:16.810
But sometimes, some of that ambiguity may
be unavoidably true.

00:35:16.810 --> 00:35:21.770
Because things don't happen the way that they
were intended during the conduct of the study,

00:35:21.770 --> 00:35:26.730
especially in a study with long-term follow-up
and with lots of unpredictable features that

00:35:26.730 --> 00:35:29.210
may emerge during its conduct.

00:35:29.210 --> 00:35:36.349
Level 4 is registration of analysis plan and
raw data, which means that I also provide

00:35:36.349 --> 00:35:43.710
the detailed information that I have collected
for others to reanalyze, repeat, and also

00:35:43.710 --> 00:35:47.460
compare notes if they try to reproduce in
a replication study.

00:35:47.460 --> 00:35:55.520
And level 5 is open live streaming where every
day in the morning, I just put online what

00:35:55.520 --> 00:35:58.950
are the experiments and what are the analyses
that I'm planning to do for the rest of the

00:35:58.950 --> 00:36:00.450
day, or I can do that overnight.

00:36:00.450 --> 00:36:05.970
I can get feedback from the whole scientific
community on whether this is a good idea and

00:36:05.970 --> 00:36:08.480
whether I could improve any of that.

00:36:08.480 --> 00:36:15.420
And then I revisit the analysis or the experimental
plan and I report the results and then I put

00:36:15.420 --> 00:36:20.160
my new set of experiments online, and everybody
can comment in a continuing basis.

00:36:20.160 --> 00:36:23.710
This is one example of a registered report.

00:36:23.710 --> 00:36:27.570
It's part of the reproducibility project,
Cancer Biology.

00:36:27.570 --> 00:36:32.700
In a way, these papers have been published
before the research was done.

00:36:32.700 --> 00:36:36.200
It's the detailed protocol of what will be
done.

00:36:36.200 --> 00:36:42.460
Practically, it can also convey what are the
tables and the figures that will be in the

00:36:42.460 --> 00:36:44.840
final paper when the results get accumulated.

00:36:44.840 --> 00:36:51.580
So everybody can see that this is the original
intention, and then can compare notes on whether

00:36:51.580 --> 00:36:53.840
the original intention was followed.

00:36:53.840 --> 00:36:56.340
And the paper is populated with the results
and published.

00:36:56.340 --> 00:37:01.690
But there is full correspondence between the
registered report before the research is done

00:37:01.690 --> 00:37:05.540
and the registered report after the research
is done.

00:37:05.540 --> 00:37:12.690
That's some empirical data here on the public
availability of research data in high impact

00:37:12.690 --> 00:37:13.690
factor journals.

00:37:13.690 --> 00:37:19.920
These are the journals that have the highest
impact factor across all science and who publish

00:37:19.920 --> 00:37:22.910
primary research.

00:37:22.910 --> 00:37:26.560
And if you see that green color, that's good
news.

00:37:26.560 --> 00:37:38.990
It means that there are editorial policies
in place for sharing information and for making

00:37:38.990 --> 00:37:45.950
information available upon request, including
protocols, and also making this a condition

00:37:45.950 --> 00:37:48.210
of publication.

00:37:48.210 --> 00:37:53.630
And lots of color means that there are these
policies in place.

00:37:53.630 --> 00:38:00.000
But the last column shows how many out of
10 of these papers actually adhere to these

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:01.730
policies in each one of these journals.

00:38:01.730 --> 00:38:06.280
And there's lots of zeros there, which means
that the policies are in place, but really

00:38:06.280 --> 00:38:07.820
implementing them is not easy.

00:38:07.820 --> 00:38:11.980
So very often many journals say, do make your
data available.

00:38:11.980 --> 00:38:13.430
We encourage you to do that.

00:38:13.430 --> 00:38:16.530
But actually, this does not happen.

00:38:16.530 --> 00:38:23.231
It's not easy to share data; and many fields
have taken the steps to make that easy, and

00:38:23.231 --> 00:38:26.110
common, and widespread.

00:38:26.110 --> 00:38:28.290
But others who have less experience probably
see it as an obstacle that is very difficult

00:38:28.290 --> 00:38:29.290
to surmount.

00:38:29.290 --> 00:38:30.290
widespread.

00:38:30.290 --> 00:38:32.260
But others who have less experience probably
see it as an obstacle that is very difficult

00:38:32.260 --> 00:38:33.260
to surmount.

00:38:33.260 --> 00:38:42.300
And there is always these open questions about
who will prepare these data to make them meaningfully

00:38:42.300 --> 00:38:45.710
available, not just throw them out.

00:38:45.710 --> 00:38:51.650
And then people would be confused and probably
would get a lot of confusing reanalysis or

00:38:51.650 --> 00:38:53.910
reproducibility checks.

00:38:53.910 --> 00:38:56.720
Then how quickly should the data become available?

00:38:56.720 --> 00:39:02.660
Is there some sort of enforcement that can
be implemented?

00:39:02.660 --> 00:39:12.210
Should there be some committee or some way
to sort out whether everybody or some particular

00:39:12.210 --> 00:39:15.550
people with particular ideas should be able
to access these data?

00:39:15.550 --> 00:39:17.760
How quickly should the data become available?

00:39:17.760 --> 00:39:18.760
Within six months?

00:39:18.760 --> 00:39:19.900
Within one year?

00:39:19.900 --> 00:39:25.150
Many projects don't really finish, and they
can be goldmines to continue to exploit with

00:39:25.150 --> 00:39:28.000
further analysis.

00:39:28.000 --> 00:39:31.349
Empirically, sharing does not happen often.

00:39:31.349 --> 00:39:36.779
This is some data covering the period from
2000 to 2014.

00:39:36.779 --> 00:39:44.310
We took a random sample of the entire PubMed,
and we could see that out of the 500 papers,

00:39:44.310 --> 00:39:47.210
about half of them included primary data.

00:39:47.210 --> 00:39:54.480
But of these 250, only one of them had a full
protocol that had been published, and this

00:39:54.480 --> 00:39:56.490
was a separate publication.

00:39:56.490 --> 00:40:01.490
And none of them had all their raw data available
for everyone to use with no restrictions.

00:40:01.490 --> 00:40:08.740
So even though there is a lot of discussion,
and even though there are fields that do have

00:40:08.740 --> 00:40:14.000
data sharing in place, most of the time this
does not happen across the broader biomedical

00:40:14.000 --> 00:40:15.000
literature.

00:40:15.000 --> 00:40:22.170
There's many efforts to try to enhance and
support and reward some of the practices that

00:40:22.170 --> 00:40:25.260
are likely to lead to better reproducibility.

00:40:25.260 --> 00:40:31.740
So once that system is the bad system that
has been implemented in the open-science framework,

00:40:31.740 --> 00:40:36.580
in several journals in psychology mostly,
but also beyond psychology has started adopting

00:40:36.580 --> 00:40:37.610
it.

00:40:37.610 --> 00:40:44.460
And basically, the papers can get badges for
openness, for having a protocol, for having

00:40:44.460 --> 00:40:47.870
pre-registered protocol, for having raw data
available.

00:40:47.870 --> 00:40:54.349
And you can see here that psychological science
journals that adopted that policy had a steep

00:40:54.349 --> 00:40:59.940
increase in the percentage of articles that
were reporting available data for other investigators

00:40:59.940 --> 00:41:00.940
to use.

00:41:00.940 --> 00:41:05.990
But obviously, we don't know what's happening
in other journals and whether people who do

00:41:05.990 --> 00:41:11.030
want to have these open and transparent and
sharing policies, eventually clustered to

00:41:11.030 --> 00:41:17.470
which the journals reward these policies and
make the journals even more poor in terms

00:41:17.470 --> 00:41:20.360
of reproducibility prerequisites.

00:41:20.360 --> 00:41:26.720
There is a lot of debate about how to enhance
reproducibility for computational methods.

00:41:26.720 --> 00:41:35.870
A few months ago, we published a set of guidelines
for reproducibility aspects that involve data,

00:41:35.870 --> 00:41:37.570
code, and workflows.

00:41:37.570 --> 00:41:43.630
And the way that we [? can ?] [? seed ?] that
is a set of different levels of reproducibility.

00:41:43.630 --> 00:41:49.510
So ideally, you would like to have everything
available and also make sure that someone

00:41:49.510 --> 00:41:53.690
has checked every single step of what is being
published.

00:41:53.690 --> 00:42:01.369
But realistically, this may not be possible,
because then you need to have the power and

00:42:01.369 --> 00:42:06.609
the workforce to be able to go through everything
and repeat everything that has been done.

00:42:06.609 --> 00:42:10.609
Therefore, you can consider different levels.

00:42:10.609 --> 00:42:14.470
And the lower level would be that someone
would at least check that

00:42:14.470 --> 00:42:18.370
the links where the data are supposed to be,
where the software is supposed to be, where

00:42:18.370 --> 00:42:22.400
the script is supposed to be-- these links
are functional.

00:42:22.400 --> 00:42:23.970
And you can get things to move.

00:42:23.970 --> 00:42:28.820
Now, whether you get exactly the same result,
that will take things to a different level.

00:42:28.820 --> 00:42:32.830
That would require more effort, and more time,
and more commitment.

00:42:32.830 --> 00:42:38.160
Better statistics and methods clearly can
improve the reproducibility record.

00:42:38.160 --> 00:42:45.060
As I mentioned earlier, we can think of improving
the transparency of availability of analysis

00:42:45.060 --> 00:42:46.060
plans.

00:42:46.060 --> 00:42:51.760
Currently, very few studies have their analysis
plans visible before they're done.

00:42:51.760 --> 00:42:56.380
I think that there is plenty of room to improve
statistical training, and literacy, and numeracy

00:42:56.380 --> 00:43:00.920
of the scientific workforce at all levels--
starting at a very early age-- better study

00:43:00.920 --> 00:43:06.089
designs, some standard features that will
promote the trustworthiness of the results.

00:43:06.089 --> 00:43:10.040
For example, randomization and blinding of
investigators in animal experiments are very

00:43:10.040 --> 00:43:12.110
easy to do, but they're not done.

00:43:12.110 --> 00:43:18.450
And finally, some checklists that investigators
in each field would agree are totally essential

00:43:18.450 --> 00:43:21.520
to improve the chances of their research being
reproducible.

00:43:21.520 --> 00:43:24.400
These are data from in-vivo studies mostly
on animals.

00:43:24.400 --> 00:43:30.000
And you see that the vast majority of them
don't use blinding for assessing outcomes.

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:34.560
And they don't use randomization-even though
that would cost nothing and would clearly

00:43:34.560 --> 00:43:37.890
enhance the chances of the research being
reproducible.

00:43:37.890 --> 00:43:42.270
Institutional practices can also improve reproducibility.

00:43:42.270 --> 00:43:48.640
Students, post-doc fellows, investigators
can be trained on practices that improve the

00:43:48.640 --> 00:43:51.589
chances of their research being reproducible.

00:43:51.589 --> 00:43:55.640
And you can see that as some sort of continued
methodological training.

00:43:55.640 --> 00:44:00.630
Currently, we teach our students, and our
fellows, and investigators mostly about subject-matter

00:44:00.630 --> 00:44:06.210
details, but less so about the core of experimental
methods, and experimental design, and safeguards

00:44:06.210 --> 00:44:10.010
that would promote the reproducibility of
what they do.

00:44:10.010 --> 00:44:15.580
And also, institutions can have institution-wide
policies that would be producible practices

00:44:15.580 --> 00:44:22.630
and maybe penalize some practices that are
clearly against the needs of reproducibility.

00:44:22.630 --> 00:44:27.500
Reporting standards can go quite a long way
in improving transparency about what has been

00:44:27.500 --> 00:44:28.500
done.

00:44:28.500 --> 00:44:33.530
There's about 300 reporting standards that
you can find in the EQUATOR web site, and

00:44:33.530 --> 00:44:38.109
each one of them pertains to one type of study
design or setting.

00:44:38.109 --> 00:44:41.820
Many people argue that even though this is
helpful, it is a bit late.

00:44:41.820 --> 00:44:46.840
Because once you have a study that has been
done, just reporting what was done-- and sometimes,

00:44:46.840 --> 00:44:51.500
what was done might have been suboptimal,
even foolish-- may not be so helpful.

00:44:51.500 --> 00:44:57.260
And it's also asking people to accept that,
oh, what I did was completely foolish.

00:44:57.260 --> 00:45:03.380
So there is a new wave of such guidance that
is focusing on protocols rather than finished

00:45:03.380 --> 00:45:04.609
studies.

00:45:04.609 --> 00:45:10.849
And you can also find in EQUATOR a number
of such efforts that focus on what do we need

00:45:10.849 --> 00:45:17.540
to build a good protocol for an x type of
design, rather than just wait to report a

00:45:17.540 --> 00:45:19.980
study that has already been completed.

00:45:19.980 --> 00:45:26.000
Finally, we need empirical research on how
to fix some of these reproducibility problems.

00:45:26.000 --> 00:45:34.720
So our metric center at Stanford is really
trying to put together efforts that are focused

00:45:34.720 --> 00:45:39.820
around improving research practices and
eventually getting more reproducible research.

00:45:39.820 --> 00:45:46.430
This covers issues of methods, of reporting,
of verification or reproducibility per se,

00:45:46.430 --> 00:45:48.840
of evaluation, and of rewards.

00:45:48.840 --> 00:45:52.619
Because all of these are interconnected in
some way or another.

00:45:52.619 --> 00:45:59.180
And there's many initiatives around the world
currently that are focusing on various aspects

00:45:59.180 --> 00:46:01.010
of that reproducibility puzzle.

00:46:01.010 --> 00:46:05.260
So there's more appearing almost on a weekly
basis.

00:46:05.260 --> 00:46:11.839
And very often we see that there is some overlap
in these initiatives-- some redundancy-- some

00:46:11.839 --> 00:46:17.570
not necessarily bad redundancy, because I
think that we need more efforts in this sphere--

00:46:17.570 --> 00:46:22.280
but also some need of coordination of all
these efforts that are happening across very

00:46:22.280 --> 00:46:26.869
different disciplines, and across different
countries, and across different settings.

00:46:26.869 --> 00:46:37.210
So at the end of the day, maybe to really
improve reproducibility, we need to make it

00:46:37.210 --> 00:46:40.849
have meaning in the reward system.

00:46:40.849 --> 00:46:46.640
So we need to find ways to re-engineer our
reward system, which is focused mostly on

00:46:46.640 --> 00:46:48.220
productivity.

00:46:48.220 --> 00:46:50.590
And I think that productivity is very nice.

00:46:50.590 --> 00:46:52.350
I have nothing against productivity.

00:46:52.350 --> 00:46:58.940
It's very good for people to be productive,
and published, and get support for their work,

00:46:58.940 --> 00:47:00.930
and publish more, and so forth.

00:47:00.930 --> 00:47:08.930
But this needs to be tied also with quality,
reproducibility, sharing, and translational

00:47:08.930 --> 00:47:10.320
impact of the research.

00:47:10.320 --> 00:47:15.180
So I use that acronym of [? PQRST, ?] sounds
like an electrocardiogram.

00:47:15.180 --> 00:47:17.970
And currently, we're focusing too much on
the p way.

00:47:17.970 --> 00:47:24.630
Well, we need to find ways to reward these
other dimensions that would increase the trustworthiness

00:47:24.630 --> 00:47:27.530
of what is being produced.

00:47:27.530 --> 00:47:30.120
Scientists do want to see that done.

00:47:30.120 --> 00:47:36.000
There's several surveys that suggest that
we do care about reproducibility, and the

00:47:36.000 --> 00:47:40.470
scientific community is not just trying to
publish and run away.

00:47:40.470 --> 00:47:44.380
We do want to get things right, eventually.

00:47:44.380 --> 00:47:49.400
It would be nice to be right at the first
place, but sometimes, we know that this is

00:47:49.400 --> 00:47:51.390
not going to happen.

00:47:51.390 --> 00:47:54.380
And science makes progress through correcting
itself.

00:47:54.380 --> 00:47:58.740
We all do care, though, about getting things
right, eventually, and having reproducible

00:47:58.740 --> 00:48:01.990
results that we can trust and also apply.

00:48:01.990 --> 00:48:08.410
I will end with a recent paper that is, I
think, a good piece of reading material and

00:48:08.410 --> 00:48:13.630
a manifesto for reproducible science that
we published three months ago in Nature Human

00:48:13.630 --> 00:48:14.630
Behavior.

00:48:14.630 --> 00:48:19.119
It's a coalition of scientists coming from
very different fields, but feeling that there

00:48:19.119 --> 00:48:26.230
are some common threats and common opportunities
around reproducibility in that circle of generating

00:48:26.230 --> 00:48:30.900
and studying hypotheses, designing, studies
conducted, studies collecting data, analyzing

00:48:30.900 --> 00:48:36.360
data, testing hypotheses, interpreting results,
publishing, and then conducting the next experiment

00:48:36.360 --> 00:48:37.360
and so forth.

00:48:37.360 --> 00:48:41.930
There are these threats that we can deal with
with reproducible practices.

00:48:41.930 --> 00:48:45.970
And these practices would depend on different
stakeholders.

00:48:45.970 --> 00:48:51.200
Sometimes scientists can make a difference;
other times it would be journals that need

00:48:51.200 --> 00:48:53.320
to take the lead.

00:48:53.320 --> 00:48:57.200
Funders can be very influential; institutions
can have their share-- and obviously, the

00:48:57.200 --> 00:49:02.500
more stakeholders you have in the game in
favor of reproducible research, the more likely

00:49:02.500 --> 00:49:04.741
it is that you would get the
stakeholders you have in the game in favor

00:49:04.741 --> 00:49:06.050
of reproducible research, the more likely
it is that you would get the results that

00:49:06.050 --> 00:49:07.050
you want.

00:49:07.050 --> 00:49:14.119
All of the stakeholders involved in science
have sometimes very different types of interest

00:49:14.119 --> 00:49:15.119
in research.

00:49:15.119 --> 00:49:21.190
Some of them want to see results being published,
so they want publishable research-- others

00:49:21.190 --> 00:49:23.450
want fundable research.

00:49:23.450 --> 00:49:28.210
Some want to see things work-- translatable
research-- and some want to make profit.

00:49:28.210 --> 00:49:34.400
There's nothing wrong with any of these four
ideas about what research should do for us.

00:49:34.400 --> 00:49:40.960
But we need to coordinate our efforts and
to have these stakeholders align so that research

00:49:40.960 --> 00:49:43.170
eventually would be reproducible.

00:49:43.170 --> 00:49:48.970
To conclude, reproducibility has different
meanings across different scientific disciplines.

00:49:48.970 --> 00:49:54.200
But eventually, all of them aim at enhancing
our trust in scientific findings.

00:49:54.200 --> 00:49:58.310
There's many possible interventions that may
improve the efficiency of research practices

00:49:58.310 --> 00:50:01.880
and the reproducibility of the scientific
literature.

00:50:01.880 --> 00:50:06.410
We know that some of them work, but we have
very little evidence about many others.

00:50:06.410 --> 00:50:12.030
So empirical meta-research would be useful,
not only to assess the prevalence of problems

00:50:12.030 --> 00:50:16.730
of reproducibility, but also to assess the
effectiveness and the potential harms of interventions

00:50:16.730 --> 00:50:19.370
that try to make research more reproducible.

00:50:19.370 --> 00:50:21.050
Thank you.

00:50:21.050 --> 00:50:23.570
Thank you, John.

00:50:23.570 --> 00:50:24.570
That was fantastic.

00:50:24.570 --> 00:50:26.790
And I really appreciate your coming this morning.

00:50:26.790 --> 00:50:31.390
We already have a million questions-- well,
at least a dozen questions in the question

00:50:31.390 --> 00:50:32.390
box.

00:50:32.390 --> 00:50:36.089
Those of you who want to pose questions to
John, please type your questions in there.

00:50:36.089 --> 00:50:40.220
I will try to select questions that I think
are going to be easier to answer, and I'll

00:50:40.220 --> 00:50:44.490
editorialize a little bit in the interest
of time, since there's not much time left

00:50:44.490 --> 00:50:45.839
this morning.

00:50:45.839 --> 00:50:50.800
First question in the question box says, "Many
of my colleague scientists state that these

00:50:50.800 --> 00:50:53.800
claims of unreproducibility are overblown.

00:50:53.800 --> 00:50:57.869
They often say that we know who does poor
work, and we know who does good work.

00:50:57.869 --> 00:51:00.260
And in the end, science is self-correcting.

00:51:00.260 --> 00:51:02.720
So should we not overreact?

00:51:02.720 --> 00:51:08.020
That by shaking the system, we could do much
more damage to the scientific process or enterprise,

00:51:08.020 --> 00:51:10.579
which is so good for society."

00:51:10.579 --> 00:51:11.579
What is your response?

00:51:11.579 --> 00:51:16.910
I guess the corollary question is, how do
we know that most meta-research papers are

00:51:16.910 --> 00:51:18.170
not false?

00:51:18.170 --> 00:51:23.550
[LAUGHS] So I think that meta-research papers
have their own share of irreproducibility,

00:51:23.550 --> 00:51:25.650
no doubt.

00:51:25.650 --> 00:51:29.760
But I think that reproducibility is at the
core of the scientific method.

00:51:29.760 --> 00:51:31.710
So it's not really something new.

00:51:31.710 --> 00:51:36.750
I think that science has always made progress
because it's focusing on better methods, better

00:51:36.750 --> 00:51:39.560
scrutiny, better ability to correct itself.

00:51:39.560 --> 00:51:43.830
So I don't see that as a new message that
is disrupting science.

00:51:43.830 --> 00:51:50.251
In a way, it is just supporting science and
offering the real value of science, which

00:51:50.251 --> 00:51:53.750
is exactly that it can correct itself.

00:51:53.750 --> 00:52:00.910
There's clearly vast differences across different
fields in terms of how much lack of reproducibility

00:52:00.910 --> 00:52:06.800
we see; and also, in terms of what it means
not to see a result being reproduced.

00:52:06.800 --> 00:52:10.810
I think that the connotations are very different;
the implications are very different.

00:52:10.810 --> 00:52:16.380
But at the end of the day, any effort to correct
a scientific record more efficiently, more

00:52:16.380 --> 00:52:20.910
quickly, more rigorously, more robustly, is
only going to

00:52:20.910 --> 00:52:24.329
make science look better, not worse.

00:52:24.329 --> 00:52:29.650
There is some school of thought that says
that, well, these are difficult times.

00:52:29.650 --> 00:52:32.322
But you know, it has always been difficult
times for science, unfortunately.

00:52:32.322 --> 00:52:37.349
Science is the best thing that has happened
to humans, but this is something that many

00:52:37.349 --> 00:52:39.180
people don't realize.

00:52:39.180 --> 00:52:44.190
So let's just forget about this and just try
to claim that science knows everything, and

00:52:44.190 --> 00:52:45.770
everything that we publish is correct.

00:52:45.770 --> 00:52:51.950
Well, this is a very dangerous path because
if we do that, there will be lots of cases

00:52:51.950 --> 00:52:57.119
of-- easily some results will be shown to
be incorrect.

00:52:57.119 --> 00:53:02.310
And then, we are just giving ammunition to
our critics that we were overconfident in

00:53:02.310 --> 00:53:05.680
results that we shouldn't have been overconfident
with.

00:53:05.680 --> 00:53:10.310
So I do believe that to defend science, we
need to defend the scientific method, not

00:53:10.310 --> 00:53:16.109
some conception of scientists being absolutely
correct all the time, which is not what science

00:53:16.109 --> 00:53:18.630
is about.

00:53:18.630 --> 00:53:27.770
OK, one of the listeners says that in fact,
we need to go back to Popper's idea of the

00:53:27.770 --> 00:53:32.751
scientific method, where we don't try to prove
hypotheses, which is impossible to do, but

00:53:32.751 --> 00:53:34.720
to eliminate alternatives.

00:53:34.720 --> 00:53:38.590
Any thoughts there?

00:53:38.590 --> 00:53:43.620
I think that this is pretty much done already
to a good extent.

00:53:43.620 --> 00:53:52.060
And I think that there's also that higher
level of integration of knowledge where indeed,

00:53:52.060 --> 00:53:56.660
we may be eliminating some hypotheses and
making others more likely.

00:53:56.660 --> 00:54:03.359
I would prefer to see things in a Bayesian
view and think of the cumulative evidence

00:54:03.359 --> 00:54:06.060
kind of affecting our priors and generating
some posterior.

00:54:06.060 --> 00:54:14.320
There are some posteriors that are extremely
strong, and I will not be convinced otherwise,

00:54:14.320 --> 00:54:19.349
unless there is amazingly extraordinary evidence
to the contrary.

00:54:19.349 --> 00:54:28.390
So you know, climate change, or tobacco killing
people, or MMR is safe.

00:54:28.390 --> 00:54:35.400
I can't think of what evidence would persuade
me that these statements are not correct.

00:54:35.400 --> 00:54:45.310
Many other statements just have some modest,
or high, or low credibility.

00:54:45.310 --> 00:54:50.340
And they have some alternatives that also
have modest, low, or high credibility.

00:54:50.340 --> 00:54:58.750
And as we accumulate more data, we can fine
tune where our beliefs emerge.

00:54:58.750 --> 00:55:04.230
And sometimes we need to act based on relatively
modest credibility.

00:55:04.230 --> 00:55:09.290
And we don't need to wait until we know something
100% to be sure and until we have eliminated

00:55:09.290 --> 00:55:10.980
every other alternative.

00:55:10.980 --> 00:55:17.990
Because what is at stake may require immediate
action, even in the face of uncertainty.

00:55:17.990 --> 00:55:24.180
But I think if we see things from that perspective,
it makes sense that we can have a gradient

00:55:24.180 --> 00:55:29.589
of certainty and uncertainty for different
types of scientific findings, hypotheses,

00:55:29.589 --> 00:55:30.589
and their alternatives.

00:55:30.589 --> 00:55:37.589
John, do you have any thoughts on how we can
apply analysis of reproducibility to studies

00:55:37.589 --> 00:55:39.320
that involve clinical data?

00:55:39.320 --> 00:55:44.170
I guess, particularly EHR data that are collected
opportunistically, not as part of a specific

00:55:44.170 --> 00:55:50.339
protocol where HIPAA may interfere with our
ability to make data public.

00:55:50.339 --> 00:55:56.170
I think that there's many opportunities for
reproducibility in this sphere.

00:55:56.170 --> 00:56:03.931
Clearly, EHR data have their own peculiarities;
but in essence, there is many datasets, many

00:56:03.931 --> 00:56:06.860
databases that depend on EHR.

00:56:06.860 --> 00:56:10.829
And some of them
may have incompatibilities against each other,

00:56:10.829 --> 00:56:14.540
but there is also some crosstalk opportunities.

00:56:14.540 --> 00:56:22.700
So there are opportunities to compare notes
across EHR platforms, and data, and analysis.

00:56:22.700 --> 00:56:28.000
And in many situations, there may be opportunities
also to compare notes of some of the inferences

00:56:28.000 --> 00:56:33.849
made from EHR data against other traditional
types of designs, including in some cases

00:56:33.849 --> 00:56:37.430
even randomized controlled trials that may
have addressed a similar question.

00:56:37.430 --> 00:56:44.680
So I think that this is a space where you
could think of a pretty robust reproducibility

00:56:44.680 --> 00:56:52.650
agenda and try to map the performance of these
datasets in comparisons within themselves,

00:56:52.650 --> 00:56:59.150
between themselves, and in comparison with
other methods of making inference of these

00:56:59.150 --> 00:57:00.150
effects.

00:57:00.150 --> 00:57:07.109
A listener noticed that you talked about reproducibility
in the biomedical and behavioral sciences.

00:57:07.109 --> 00:57:13.040
What can you tell us about physical sciences
or chemical sciences?

00:57:13.040 --> 00:57:19.790
So, there is also work done on reproducibility
and related [? themes ?] in the physical sciences--

00:57:19.790 --> 00:57:26.800
probably less compared to social sciences
and biomedical sciences-- but we do have several

00:57:26.800 --> 00:57:28.780
pieces of evidence.

00:57:28.780 --> 00:57:34.120
The general perception is that physics and
chemistry have higher reproducibility, and

00:57:34.120 --> 00:57:35.750
to a large extent this is true.

00:57:35.750 --> 00:57:39.530
There are some simple reasons for that.

00:57:39.530 --> 00:57:43.109
Usually, there is stronger experimental design.

00:57:43.109 --> 00:57:47.300
There is more determinism in their data.

00:57:47.300 --> 00:57:50.690
They have a stronger history of transparency
and sharing.

00:57:50.690 --> 00:57:56.190
So for example, everything in high energy
and particle physics practically shared with

00:57:56.190 --> 00:58:01.390
a large community of tens of thousands of
physicists who can look at the and eventually,

00:58:01.390 --> 00:58:04.310
they all publish their results together.

00:58:04.310 --> 00:58:08.240
They have very stringent statistical rules
for claiming discoveries.

00:58:08.240 --> 00:58:13.310
So they have a five sigma rule that is equivalent
to a p-value of 10 to the minus 7, which is

00:58:13.310 --> 00:58:19.130
very close to the genome-wide level of significance
that is used in biomedicine that has worked

00:58:19.130 --> 00:58:23.180
pretty well in genome association studies
for example.

00:58:23.180 --> 00:58:30.500
So there's many reasons why you see a pretty
good reproducibility in the sciences.

00:58:30.500 --> 00:58:33.359
There's also great tolerance for negative
results.

00:58:33.359 --> 00:58:40.470
Most of the results in experimental physics
are negative, practically, and nobody worries

00:58:40.470 --> 00:58:41.859
about that.

00:58:41.859 --> 00:58:45.800
You see all these papers coming out from CERN,
hundreds of papers, they have found one higgs

00:58:45.800 --> 00:58:48.190
boson and pretty much nothing else.

00:58:48.190 --> 00:58:52.381
That was a great discovery, but I would say
it's an equally great discovery that all the

00:58:52.381 --> 00:58:57.140
other results are negative, and people just
say that this is what they are.

00:58:57.140 --> 00:59:03.770
There are still some problems in specific
fields-- that the experimentation or the observation

00:59:03.770 --> 00:59:04.890
is more difficult.

00:59:04.890 --> 00:59:12.369
For example, claims for exoplanets-- about
50% of the time they turn out to be non-reproducible--

00:59:12.369 --> 00:59:16.670
range from 25% to 60% in different surveys.

00:59:16.670 --> 00:59:24.190
Some of the more recent wave of chemical synthesis
have been a bit difficult to reproduce [? in

00:59:24.190 --> 00:59:27.920
?] [? dependent ?] labs, but I hope that this
is the exception.

00:59:27.920 --> 00:59:33.859
So no scientific field is immune to these
problems because these are not necessarily

00:59:33.859 --> 00:59:36.589
problems, this is the scientific method.

00:59:36.589 --> 00:59:40.720
And you know, it's difficult to do good science.

00:59:40.720 --> 00:59:42.760
That's great.

00:59:42.760 --> 00:59:44.270
And on that note, we should end this.

00:59:44.270 --> 00:59:45.410
We've reached the end of the hour.

00:59:45.410 --> 00:59:48.630
John, let me thank you for what was a really
stimulating talk.

00:59:48.630 --> 00:59:51.030
And then let me thank all the people who dialed
in this morning.

00:59:51.030 --> 00:59:53.510
It was really great to hear you.

00:59:53.510 --> 00:59:54.720
Thanks again.

00:59:54.720 --> 00:59:55.940
Thank you.

